## Conclusion

A wedding day's sound is not one setup, it is several, run by the same technician from the first guest to the last dance: a soundcheck, a lavalier microphone for the vows, a handheld microphone for speeches, a reset between your ceremony and reception spaces if they differ, and a volume shift into dancing. [Contact us](/contact/) to talk through your ceremony and reception. For our complete audio visual rental range in Malaga, see the [audio visual rental](/blog/audio-visual-rental/) guide.

## FAQs

### What microphone do you use for the vows, and who wears it?

A wireless lavalier microphone, clipped to the officiant. It is small, stays out of photos, and does not require either of you to stand near a microphone stand during your vows. See [During the Vows](#during-the-vows-the-officiants-microphone-and-why-it-needs-a-technician) above.

### Why does a lavalier microphone need a technician present?

A lavalier microphone, worn close to the mouth and run through a live PA system, is genuinely more prone to feedback than a handheld microphone held further from the speakers. The Wedding Pack includes a technician for the full event specifically to watch for that risk and adjust before your guests notice. See [During the Vows](#during-the-vows-the-officiants-microphone-and-why-it-needs-a-technician) above.

### What happens to the sound setup between the ceremony and the reception?

Our technician already adjusts sound continuously as your day moves from ceremony to speeches to dancing. When that also means a change of room, the same room by room principle applies: a space's acoustics change what correct settings look like, which is exactly why the Wedding Pack technician has no hour cap. See [The Ceremony to Reception Switch](#the-ceremony-to-reception-switch-when-your-venue-changes-mid-event) above.

### Is there a soundcheck before the ceremony starts?

Yes. Our crew tests the officiant's microphone and the ceremony speakers against the actual venue before your first guest is seated, not hours earlier in an empty room. See [Before the Ceremony](#before-the-ceremony-soundcheck-and-setup) above.

### Does the technician stay for speeches and dancing too, or just the ceremony?

The whole event, with no hour cap, one of only two packages in our catalog built that way. The same technician switches from the officiant's lavalier to a handheld microphone for speeches, then brings the volume up for dancing later in the night. See [Speeches at the Reception](#speeches-at-the-reception-the-handheld-microphone-and-the-handoff) and [Dancing](#dancing-when-the-volume-changes) above.
